hls: a playlist that starts over is joined again

A live playlist moves forward: each time it is fetched it begins with
the same sequence number or a higher one. When the origin behind it is
started again, it counts its segments from where it pleases, as a rule
from nought, and its timestamps start over with them.

The reader was waiting for the segment after the last one it read, by
number. In the new list that number is days away. It logged that the
sequence had changed unexpectedly and went on fetching the playlist,
max_reload times, which with six-second segments is five minutes, and
then took the stream to have ended. All that while the channel was on
the air again.

A list that has merely fallen behind, as that of one server among
several may, still reaches the number the list before it began with. A
list whose segments all lie before that number is another list. The
reader now keeps the number the list began with when it last listed
anything, so that an answer without segments in between does not hide
the change, and joins a list that ends before it where a reader opening
it at that moment would: live_start_index from its end.

The timestamps of the new segments are far below the first ones the
reader saw, and were taken for a counter that had gone round: in a
transport stream every one of them had 2^33 added, twenty-six and a half
hours. Once a list has started over, timestamps are passed on as they
come, and the caller sees them go back, which is what they did.

